tests-asn1c-compiler - asn1c produces expected textual output
tests-asn1c-smoke    - asn1c compiler produces compilable code for simple types
tests-c-compiler     - C code compiles and runs, and produces expected result
tests-skeletons      - "skeletons" code primitives are correct
tests-randomized     - fuzz-testing of DER/OER/PER/XER codecs

Randomized corpus coverage (local, opt-in)
----------------------------------------
Ordinary randomized checks keep encode/decode and copy tests, but skip corpus
files when LibFuzzer is disabled. GENERATE_CORPUS=auto is the default: fuzzing
automatically generates its seeds. GENERATE_CORPUS=yes also tests generation
without fuzzing; GENERATE_CORPUS=no is rejected when LibFuzzer is enabled.

From a configured build tree, run all corpus cases with a shared job budget:

    make -C tests/tests-randomized -j12 check-corpus

This local-only target splits every bundle into one-line cases, runs their
round-trip/copy and corpus-generation checks, and disables timed fuzzing even
in a fuzzer-enabled build. It retains both existing compiler-mode passes and
any configured 32-bit pass. Cases and inner builds share the caller's job
budget; they do not each start another 12-job pool. On the 10-core machine,
compare -j10, -j12, and -j20 using fresh invocations. No fixed speedup is promised.

Runtime conjecture for the Intel i9-10910 (10 physical / 20 logical CPUs,
64 GiB RAM): initially budget about 3-6 hours at -j12 for the complete corpus
target, and allow up to 8 hours for the first measured run. This is an unverified
planning estimate, not a benchmark or a timeout. The October 12 baseline spent
about 5 hours 27 minutes in randomized tests with -j8, including corpus
generation. More evenly scheduled individual cases and shared parallel builds
should shorten the serial tail, but large collection cases, sanitizers, filesystem
overhead, and repeated runtime compilation still cost time. There are currently
289 source cases (578 native-mode executions before any optional 32-bit passes).
An enabled 32-bit pass increases the work; compiler caching and other future
build fixes can change the estimate substantially. The focused two-bundle command
below checks setup but is not representative of the large collection cases.

Measure the complete target independently of the rest of the test suite:

    time make -C tests/tests-randomized -j12 check-corpus

Record the compiler, sanitizer settings, job limit, cache state, and elapsed
time alongside the retained logs before replacing this conjecture with a measured
budget. Do not infer a 20/12 speedup from using 20 logical CPUs.

A focused run is available by selecting bundles:

    make -C tests/tests-randomized -j12 check-corpus \
        TESTS='bundles/00-NULL-bundle.txt bundles/08-OBJECT-IDENTIFIER-bundle.txt'

Each invocation creates unique cases and logs in .tmp.corpus-cases.* below the
randomized build directory, avoiding cached results from ordinary checks.
Generated seed files remain in the corresponding .tmp.corpus-* case directories
(the final compiler-mode pass is retained). Use make clean to remove these files.
The target is not part of make check or the standard GitHub workflows. Cheap
mock-tool policy regressions still run as part of ordinary make check.

For the usual bundle-based tests, GENERATE_CORPUS=yes can also be supplied to
make check. Success markers distinguish corpus and fuzz modes, so a prior
non-corpus result cannot satisfy a corpus or fuzz request. Automake's existing
make check path reruns the drivers, which then check the matching success marker.
The local check-corpus target always creates fresh case names.
For a standalone check-bundles.sh invocation, -j N explicitly selects inner
build parallelism; otherwise it inherits the calling make jobserver.

